      "text": "**Why \"split ergativity\":** The language is nominative-accusative in most contexts (the typologically common pattern: subject always gets nominative, verb agrees with subject) but switches to ergative-absolutive in perfective transitive contexts (the typologically rarer pattern: the agent of a transitive verb gets marked, and the verb agrees with the patient instead). The \"split\" is the trigger condition: aspect × transitivity."
